s($,"cl1","bNv",()=>{var q="\ud83c\udf2c\ufe0f",p="5 min",o="3 min",n="10 min",m="Self-Compassion Break",l="Ongoing",k="15 min",j=A.aH("O<adu>"),i=t.s,h=A.aH("O<adt>")
return A.ah(["anxiety",A.tg("Cognitive Behavioral Therapy helps you identify and reframe anxious thoughts. ACT teaches you to hold thoughts without being controlled by them. Together they're the most evidence-backed approach for anxiety.","CBT + Acceptance & Commitment Therapy (ACT)",B.h,"\ud83d\ude0c","anxiety",'Use CBT principles \u2014 help identify automatic negative thoughts, label cognitive distortions (catastrophising, mind-reading, etc.), and guide gentle cognitive restructuring. Apply ACT defusion language: "I notice I\'m having the thought that...". Never dismiss the anxiety \u2014 acknowledge it physiologically first.',A.a([A.cT(B.lM,"Inhale for 4, hold for 7, exhale for 8 seconds. Activates your parasympathetic system \u2014 the physiological off-switch for anxiety.",p,q,"Activates the vagus nerve, reducing cortisol within 2\u20134 cycles.","4-7-8 Breathing","Any time anxiety spikes. Before exams, presentations, or difficult conversations."),A.cT(B.jW,"Name 5 things you see, 4 you can touch, 3 you hear, 2 you smell, 1 you taste. Anchors your mind to the present, breaking the anxiety cycle.",o,"\ud83c\udf0d","Sensory grounding interrupts the amygdala's threat response loop.","5-4-3-2-1 Grounding","During panic or spiralling thoughts. Anywhere, discreetly."),A.cT(B.d_,"Write the anxious thought \u2192 identify the cognitive distortion (catastrophising? mind-reading?) \u2192 generate a balanced, evidence-based alternative.",n,"\ud83e\udde0","Core CBT technique \u2014 changes neural pathways over 4\u20138 weeks.","Cognitive Restructuring","When a specific worry keeps returning. Use your journal."),A.cT(B.d_,"Dedicate 15 minutes daily to all your worries. When anxiety appears outside that window, note it and defer it. This contains worry rather than suppressing it.","15 min/day","\u23f0","Reduces intrusive thoughts by 40% in controlled studies.","Worry Time Scheduling","Daily \u2014 pick a fixed time (e.g., 5pm). Not before bed."),A.cT(B.d_,'Instead of "I\'m anxious", say "I notice I\'m having the thought that I\'m anxious." Creates psychological distance between you and your thoughts.',"2 min","\ud83c\udf31","ACT defusion reduces anxiety's power without requiring you to change the thought.","Thought Defusion (ACT)","When thoughts feel overwhelming or all-consuming."),A.cT(B.aWw,"Tense each muscle group for 5 seconds, then release for 30. Start from your feet, work upward. Releases physically stored anxiety.",n,"\ud83d\udc86","Reduces somatic anxiety symptoms by releasing held muscle tension.","Progressive Muscle Relaxation","Before bed or after a stressful day. Lying down is best.")],j),"Managing Anxiety",A.a([A.dP(A.a(["Practice 4-7-8 breathing once daily","Journal: where does anxiety live in your body?","Use 5-4-3-2-1 grounding when worried"],i),"Learn to notice anxiety in your body before your mind spirals.","Build Body Awareness",1),A.dP(A.a(["Log one anxious thought per day in your journal","Schedule your daily worry time (15 min)","Add PMR to your evening routine"],i),"Start identifying the thoughts that trigger anxiety.","Catch the Thoughts",2),A.dP(A.a(["Restructure 1 thought per day (journal)","Practice thought defusion during worry time","Reduce breathing to 3 cycles before stressors"],i),"Apply cognitive restructuring to your most frequent anxious thoughts.","Challenge & Reframe",3),A.dP(A.a(["Identify your 2 most effective techniques","Create a personal anxiety protocol card","Reflect on what has changed in your body and thoughts"],i),"Make anxiety tools automatic \u2014 use them without thinking.","Sustain & Integrate",4)],h)),"depression",A.tg("Behavioural Activation is as effective as antidepressants for mild-moderate depression. It breaks the cycle of inactivity \u2192 low mood \u2192 more inactivity. Self-compassion work counters the harsh inner critic depression amplifies.","Behavioural Activation (BA) + Self-Compassion",B.p0,"\ud83d\udc99","depression",'Use Behavioural Activation language \u2014 focus on action before feeling, not waiting to feel motivated. Validate the weight of low mood without reinforcing hopelessness. Offer micro-commitments ("just one thing"). Apply self-compassion language (Kristin Neff) when self-criticism appears. Avoid toxic positivity \u2014 "it makes sense you feel this way" before any reframe.',A.a([A.cT(B.cT,"Schedule one small, meaningful activity before you feel like doing it \u2014 not after. Depression lies and says motivation comes before action. It's the other way around.","5\u201330 min","\ud83d\udeb6","BA is as effective as CBT and medication for mild-moderate depression.","Behavioural Activation","Every day. Start with 5 minutes of something you once enjoyed."),A.cT(B.cT,"Identify what depression tells you to do (stay in bed, isolate, avoid) \u2014 then do the opposite. Not with full force, but with one small step in the other direction.",p,"\ud83d\udd04","DBT Opposite Action reduces the intensity and duration of depressive episodes.","Opposite Action (DBT)","When the urge to withdraw or shut down is strongest."),A.cT(B.d_,"Write 3 specific things that went okay today \u2014 even tiny ones. Then spend 30 seconds actually feeling each one. Savoring rewires the brain's negativity bias.",p,"\ud83c\udf38","Sustained gratitude practice increases positive affect over 6\u20138 weeks.","Gratitude & Savoring","Morning or before bed. Consistency over intensity."),A.cT(B.jW,'1. Acknowledge the suffering: "This is really hard." 2. Remind yourself it\'s human: "Everyone struggles." 3. Offer kindness: "May I be gentle with myself right now." Based on Kristin Neff\'s research.',o,"\ud83e\udd17","Self-compassion reduces depression symptoms independently of self-esteem levels.",m,"When self-criticism is loud. When you feel like a failure."),A.cT(B.cT,"Schedule one human interaction today \u2014 a message, a phone call, showing up somewhere. Depression thrives in isolation. Connection is medicine.","15\u201330 min","\ud83d\udc65","Social connection is the single strongest predictor of mood recovery.","Connection Scheduling","Daily. Even a short text counts. Lower the bar to near zero."),A.cT(B.d_,"Identify one of your core values (family, creativity, learning) \u2192 do one small action aligned with it today. Purpose-driven action is the most powerful antidepressant.",n,"\ud83c\udfaf","Values-based action is the primary mechanism in ACT for depression.","Values Activation",'Weekly planning. Ask: "What matters to me, and did I touch it today?"')],j),"Coping with Low Mood",A.a([A.dP(A.a(["Pick ONE activity per day \u2014 5 minutes only",'Write one thing that was okay (not "good") each evening',"Tell Maya how you're actually feeling each day"],i),"Any action is a win. Start absurdly small.","Lower the Bar",1),A.dP(A.a(["Add a second micro-activity to your day","Begin a daily gratitude + savoring practice","Schedule one human connection this week"],i),"Small actions are starting to compound.","Build Momentum",2),A.dP(A.a(["Practice self-compassion breaks when self-criticism appears","Journal: what would I say to a friend feeling this?","Apply Opposite Action to one avoidance pattern"],i),"Address the self-critical thoughts that fuel low mood.","Counter the Inner Critic",3),A.dP(A.a(["Identify your top 3 values","Do one values-aligned activity","Review: what worked? What helped most?"],i),"Ground your actions in what truly matters to you.","Connect to Values",4)],h)),"stress",A.tg("SIT teaches you to prepare for and work through stressors in graduated steps. Problem-Solving Therapy breaks overwhelming situations into solvable chunks. Both are highly effective for academic environments.","Stress Inoculation Training + Problem-Solving Therapy",B.y,"\ud83d\udcda","stress","Use Problem-Solving Therapy framing: break overwhelming situations into small, solvable steps. Acknowledge Kenyan academic pressures specifically (CATs, HELB, attachment). Validate the objective difficulty before offering strategy. Use Stress Inoculation \u2014 help them mentally prepare for difficult scenarios rather than avoid thinking about them.",A.a([A.cT(B.d_,"Write every task on your mind \u2014 no filter. Then categorise: Urgent+Important (do now), Important+Not Urgent (schedule), Urgent+Not Important (delegate or minimize), Neither (drop).",n,"\ud83d\uddc2\ufe0f","Externalising cognitive load reduces perceived stress by up to 30%.","Brain Dump + Priority Matrix","Every Sunday evening or when overwhelmed by your to-do list."),A.cT(B.cT,"Work with full focus for 25 minutes \u2192 5-minute break. After 4 cycles, take a 30-minute break. Trains focus, prevents burnout, and makes large tasks feel manageable.","25/5 min cycles","\ud83c\udf45","Regular breaks restore cognitive resources and prevent decision fatigue.","Pomodoro Technique","Any study session. Especially effective before CATs."),A.cT(B.lM,"Inhale for 4, hold for 4, exhale for 4, hold for 4. Navy SEAL protocol for regulating stress in high-pressure performance situations.","4 min",q,"Box breathing reduces cortisol and increases pre-frontal cortex activity.","Box Breathing","Before exams, CATs, presentations, or when overwhelm hits mid-study."),A.cT(B.cT,"Plan study in blocks with deliberate rest. Cognitive performance peaks at 90-minute ultradian cycles. Work with your biology, not against it.",l,"\u26a1","Ultradian rhythms mean rest is not laziness \u2014 it's required for consolidation.","Study-Rest Cycles","Design your study schedule around 90-minute deep work blocks."),A.cT(B.d_,'Separate task performance from self-worth. Your exam result is not a verdict on your value as a person. Write: "The worst realistic outcome is X. I could handle X because..."',p,"\ud83e\udde0","Decoupling identity from performance reduces test anxiety significantly.","Cognitive Decoupling","When stress is really about what failure would mean about you.")],j),"Managing Academic Stress",A.a([A.dP(A.a(["Do a brain dump \u2014 every task and worry on paper","Build a simple priority matrix for the week","Use box breathing before each study session"],i),"Name everything that is stressing you \u2014 then organise it.","Get Clear",1),A.dP(A.a(["Start Pomodoro for all study sessions","Build in deliberate rest after 90-minute blocks","Identify your peak focus hours and protect them"],i),"Structure study to match how your brain actually works.","Work Smarter",2),A.dP(A.a(["Cognitive decoupling: separate performance from identity","Stress inoculation: mentally rehearse challenging scenarios","Add movement to your day \u2014 it processes cortisol physically"],i),"Build stress resilience, not just stress management.","Mental Stress Fitness",3),A.dP(A.a(["Protect sleep \u2014 it consolidates everything you studied","Design a post-CAT/exam recovery ritual","Identify your stress early-warning signs for next semester"],i),"Sustainable study requires deliberate recovery.","Protect Your Recovery",4)],h)),"sleep",A.tg("CBT-I is the gold-standard treatment for insomnia \u2014 more effective than sleep medication with no side effects. It addresses the thoughts and behaviours that perpetuate poor sleep, rather than just the symptom.","CBT-I (Cognitive Behavioural Therapy for Insomnia)",B.a3I,"\ud83d\ude34","sleep",'Apply CBT-I principles. Avoid reinforcing sleep effort \u2014 striving to sleep makes it harder. Use paradoxical intention language: "try to stay awake with your eyes closed". Emphasise the stimulus control and consistent wake time as the two highest-leverage changes. Validate how profoundly sleep deprivation affects mood, concentration, and stress.',A.a([A.cT(B.cT,"Keep a consistent wake time every day (including weekends). Make your bed only for sleep. Keep your room cool (18-20\xb0C), dark, and quiet. No screens 45 minutes before bed.",l,"\ud83c\udf19","A consistent wake time is the single most powerful regulator of your circadian rhythm.","Sleep Hygiene Protocol","Every day. Consistency is the foundation of good sleep."),A.cT(B.cT,"Use your bed ONLY for sleep (and intimacy). If you can't sleep after 20 minutes, get up and do something quiet in dim light until you're sleepy. Return to bed only when drowsy.",l,"\ud83d\udecf\ufe0f","Stimulus control retrains the bed-sleep association within 2\u20133 weeks.","Stimulus Control","Every night \u2014 especially when you notice yourself lying awake thinking."),A.cT(B.lM,"Inhale for 4, hold for 7, exhale for 8. Do 4 cycles lying down, lights off. Slows heart rate and signals the nervous system that sleep is safe.",p,q,"Activates the parasympathetic system \u2014 physiologically prepares the body for sleep.","4-7-8 Pre-Sleep Breathing","In bed, as your final pre-sleep activity."),A.cT(B.cT,"Create a consistent 30-minute pre-sleep sequence: dim lights, stop screens, do something calm (reading, stretching, journaling worries). Your brain learns that this sequence ends in sleep.","30 min","\ud83d\udd6f\ufe0f","Conditioned sleep rituals reduce sleep onset latency by signalling the brain.","Wind-Down Ritual","30\u201345 minutes before your target sleep time. Every night."),A.cT(B.d_,"At 9pm (not in bed), write all your worries and tomorrow's tasks. Tell your brain: \"It's written down. You don't need to keep hold of it.\" Then close the notebook.",n,"\ud83d\udcdd","Pre-sleep worry journaling reduces cognitive arousal \u2014 the primary cause of insomnia.","Worry Dump Pre-Bed","Before starting your wind-down ritual. Keep the notebook outside the bedroom."),A.cT(B.cT,"Temporarily restrict your time in bed to your actual sleep time (e.g., if you sleep 5hrs, only spend 5hrs in bed). Builds sleep pressure that drives deeper, more efficient sleep. Gradually extend as efficiency improves.","2\u20134 weeks","\u23f1\ufe0f","Most powerful CBT-I technique \u2014 80%+ efficacy for chronic insomnia.","Sleep Restriction Therapy","Under guidance \u2014 for chronic insomnia. Discuss with Maya first.")],j),"Better Sleep",A.a([A.dP(A.a(["Set the same wake time every day for 7 days","Create a 30-minute wind-down sequence","Stop screens 45 minutes before bed"],i),"Set a fixed wake time and build your wind-down ritual.","Establish Your Anchors",1),A.dP(A.a(["Implement stimulus control \u2014 bed only for sleep","Start the pre-bed worry dump nightly","Add 4-7-8 breathing as your sleep trigger"],i),"Make your bedroom a sleep sanctuary.","Control Your Sleep Environment",2),A.dP(A.a(["Journal: what beliefs do you have about sleep?","Challenge catastrophic sleep thoughts (CBT)","Track sleep quality alongside mood in MindBridge"],i),"Challenge beliefs that keep you awake.","Address Sleep Thoughts",3),A.dP(A.a(["Identify your top 3 sleep disruptors","Build a weekend sleep strategy (don't oversleep)","Reflect: how has your mood changed with better sleep?"],i),"Sleep health is ongoing \u2014 identify your personal sleep threats.","Protect Your Progress",4)],h)),"social",A.tg("Social anxiety responds best to graduated exposure \u2014 approaching feared situations in increasing order of difficulty. Combined with social skills practice, this builds genuine confidence from the ground up.","Social Skills Training + Graduated Exposure",B.dK,"\ud83e\udd1d","social","Use graduated exposure language \u2014 never push too fast, but gently challenge avoidance. Celebrate any social action, no matter how small. Apply post-event processing correction explicitly. Normalise social anxiety as the brain's threat system doing its job, not a personality flaw. Use DEAR MAN framing when the user needs to have a difficult conversation.",A.a([A.cT(B.cT,"List social situations from least to most anxiety-provoking. Start at the bottom \u2014 stay in each until your anxiety drops by 50%. Move up only when ready. Avoidance maintains social anxiety; exposure reduces it.",l,"\ud83e\ude9c","Graduated exposure is the most effective treatment for social anxiety disorder.","Exposure Hierarchy","As your weekly practice \u2014 one rung per week minimum."),A.cT(B.cT,"Describe the situation \u2192 Express feelings \u2192 Assert what you want \u2192 Reinforce the other person \u2192 stay Mindful \u2192 Appear confident \u2192 Negotiate. A structured framework for effective communication.","5 min to learn","\ud83d\udde3\ufe0f","DBT interpersonal effectiveness \u2014 proven in clinical trials for social functioning.","DEAR MAN (DBT)","Before any conversation where you want to express a need or set a limit."),A.cT(B.d_,"After a social interaction, our bias is to replay only what went wrong. Force yourself to list 3 things that went okay \u2014 regardless of how small. Correct the negative post-event filter.",p,"\ud83d\udd0d","Negative post-event processing maintains social anxiety \u2014 correcting it disrupts the cycle.","Post-Event Processing Correction","Within 30 minutes after any social interaction you feel anxious about."),A.cT(B.lM,"Do 4 cycles of box breathing (4-4-4-4) immediately before any social situation. Reduces visible anxiety symptoms and increases confidence.",o,q,"Physiological regulation reduces the anxiety signals others misread as disinterest.","Box Breathing Before Social","5 minutes before entering social situations, making calls, or speaking up."),A.cT(B.cT,"Small daily connection challenges: smile at someone, ask a classmate one question, send one message you've been drafting. Frequency of small connections builds social fluency.","1\u20135 min","\u2728","Frequent, low-stakes social practice is more effective than occasional high-stakes attempts.","Connection Micro-Challenges","Daily. Pick one micro-challenge each morning.")],j),"Social Confidence",A.a([A.dP(A.a(["List 10 social situations ranked by anxiety (0\u201310)","Start with rungs 1\u20133 this week","Practice post-event processing after each"],i),"Build your personal social hierarchy from easiest to hardest.","Map Your Exposure Ladder",1),A.dP(A.a(["One micro-challenge per day (smile, ask, text)","Learn DEAR MAN \u2014 role play it with Maya","Box breathing before any social situation this week"],i),"Build social muscle through frequent low-stakes practice.","Daily Micro-Connections",2),A.dP(A.a(["Move to rungs 4\u20136 on your exposure ladder","Apply DEAR MAN in one real situation","Journal: what surprised you positively this week?"],i),"Approach situations that felt too scary in week 1.","Climb the Ladder",3),A.dP(A.a(["Reflect on your progress on the exposure ladder","Challenge yourself to one conversation you've avoided","Rewrite your social narrative: who are you becoming?"],i),"Social confidence is built through repetition, not breakthroughs.","Consolidate & Push",4)],h)),"motivation",A.tg('Motivational Interviewing helps you clarify your own "why" \u2014 sustainable motivation must come from within. BJ Fogg\'s Tiny Habits model shows that behaviour change succeeds through small wins, not willpower.',"Motivational Interviewing + Habit Formation (BJ Fogg)",B.c9,"\ud83d\ude80","motivation","Use Motivational Interviewing \u2014 explore ambivalence without pushing. Evoke motivation from the user's own values and goals. Use reflective listening and importance/confidence rulers. Apply BJ Fogg language: celebrate tiny wins enthusiastically. Never shame lack of motivation \u2014 normalise it and redirect to structure.",A.a([A.cT(B.d_,"List your top 5 values (family, growth, creativity, freedom, etc.) \u2192 rank them \u2192 ask: is what I'm working toward aligned with what I actually value? Motivation flows naturally from values alignment.",k,"\ud83c\udfaf","Intrinsic motivation (values-driven) outlasts extrinsic motivation by 3\u20135x.","Values Clarification","Monthly or whenever motivation feels hollow."),A.cT(B.cT,'"When [situation], I will [behaviour] for [duration]." E.g., "When my 8am alarm rings, I will open my notes for 10 minutes." Specificity triples follow-through rates.',p,"\ud83d\udcc5","Increased goal follow-through by 2\u20133x in meta-analyses.","Implementation Intentions","Every Sunday \u2014 plan your week's key intentions."),A.cT(B.cT,'Scale any habit down to 2 minutes. "Read for 30 minutes" \u2192 "open the book." "Exercise for an hour" \u2192 "put on my shoes." Starting is the hardest part. Two minutes always wins over nothing.',"2 min start","\u26a1","Identity-based habits succeed when the barrier to entry is removed.","Tiny Habits (2-Minute Rule)","For any habit you keep failing to start."),A.cT(B.cT,'Pair something you want to do (music, podcast, coffee) with something you need to do (study, exercise, admin). Only have the "want" when doing the "need".',l,"\ud83c\udfb5","Increases engagement with aversive tasks by making them conditionally rewarding.","Temptation Bundling","For recurring tasks you procrastinate on."),A.cT(B.cT,'Track streaks visually \u2014 your MindBridge streak IS this technique. "Don\'t break the chain" is a powerful motivational heuristic. One day at a time.',"1 min/day","\ud83d\udd25","Visual progress tracking increases intrinsic motivation and task completion.","Momentum Tracking","Daily. Check your streak. Let it motivate the next check-in.")],j),"Boosting Motivation",A.a([A.dP(A.a(["Complete a values clarification exercise",'Write your "motivation statement" \u2014 one honest sentence',"Identify the one habit that would change everything"],i),"Sustainable motivation starts with clarity about what you actually want.","Find Your Why",1),A.dP(A.a(["Identify your most important habit this week","Scale it to 2 minutes \u2014 commit to JUST that","Create your implementation intention for it"],i),"Shrink your most important habit to its 2-minute version.","Make It Tiny",2),A.dP(A.a(["Habit stack: after [existing routine], I will [new habit]","Bundle one temptation with one important task","Track your consecutive days \u2014 protect your streak"],i),"Attach your habits to things you already do.","Stack and Bundle",3),A.dP(A.a(['Reframe: from goal to identity ("I am a person who studies consistently")',"Journal: what evidence do I have for this identity this month?","Plan next month with your motivation insight"],i),'"I am someone who..." is more powerful than "I want to..."',"Become the Identity",4)],h)),"mindfulness",A.tg("MBSR is one of the most researched wellness interventions. 8 weeks of practice produces measurable changes in brain structure \u2014 more grey matter in the prefrontal cortex, smaller amygdala reactivity.","MBSR (Mindfulness-Based Stress Reduction \u2014 Kabat-Zinn)",B.aS,"\ud83e\uddd8","mindfulness","Use MBSR-informed language \u2014 never teach mindfulness, just demonstrate it. Respond mindfully (pause, reflect, present). Invite the user into present-moment awareness during conversations. Validate that a wandering mind is not failure \u2014 the noticing is the practice. Use sensory language to anchor.",A.a([A.cT(B.jW,"Stop \u2192 Take a breath \u2192 Observe (thoughts, feelings, body) \u2192 Proceed mindfully. The most practical mindfulness tool for a busy student life.","1 min","\ud83d\uded1","Brief mindful pauses reduce cortisol and restore attentional focus.","STOP Practice","Any transition point: between classes, before meals, when stress spikes."),A.cT(B.lM,"Double-inhale through the nose (short + extend), then long exhale through mouth. This deflates the alveoli and is the fastest physiological way to reduce stress.","15 seconds","\ud83d\ude2e\u200d\ud83d\udca8","Faster stress reduction than any other breathing technique (Huberman Lab, Stanford).","Physiological Sigh","Any moment. It works in 15 seconds \u2014 no one needs to know you're doing it."),A.cT(B.jW,"Sit comfortably, close eyes, focus on breath. When attention wanders (it will), notice it and return gently. The noticing and returning IS the practice \u2014 not staying focused.","10\u201320 min","\ud83e\uddd8","Daily practice for 8 weeks measurably alters brain structure.","Formal Sitting Meditation","Same time every day \u2014 morning is best for most people."),A.cT(B.jW,"Slowly move attention from toes to crown, noticing sensations without judgement. Reconnects mind and body \u2014 particularly powerful for those who dissociate under stress.",k,"\ud83d\udd0d","Body scan is one of the most effective MBSR techniques for reducing rumination.","Body Scan","Before sleep, or when feeling disconnected from your body."),A.cT(B.jW,"Walk slowly, feeling each step \u2014 heel to toe. Notice sights, sounds, air. Leave your phone in your pocket. Campus walks become meditation sessions.",n,"\ud83d\udeb6","Mindful walking reduces ruminative thinking and restores directed attention.","Mindful Walking","Between classes, during lunch, or whenever you have a few minutes outside.")],j),"Mindfulness Practice",A.a([A.dP(A.a(["Practice the STOP technique 3\xd7 daily","One 5-minute sitting session per day","Try physiological sigh when stress hits"],i),"Awareness starts with the one thing always with you \u2014 your breath.","Begin with Breath",1),A.dP(A.a(["Add a body scan before sleep","One mindful walk per day (no phone)","Extend sitting sessions to 10 minutes"],i),"Move beyond breath to body and senses.","Expand Awareness",2),A.dP(A.a(["Eat one meal per day without screens or reading","Practice STOP at every transition (class to class)","Extend to 15-minute daily sitting practice"],i),"Bring mindfulness into ordinary activities.","Mindfulness in Daily Life",3),A.dP(A.a(["Identify your 2 core techniques","Create a daily mindfulness anchor (same time, same space)","Reflect: what has changed in how you relate to stress?"],i),"Build a personal practice that fits your life.","Sustain the Practice",4)],h)),"selfesteem",A.tg("Schema Therapy addresses the deep core beliefs (schemas) that drive low self-esteem \u2014 often formed in childhood or difficult life experiences. Self-compassion work offers an evidence-based, sustainable alternative to fragile self-esteem.","Schema Therapy + Self-Compassion (Kristin Neff)",B.dK,"\ud83d\udcaa","selfesteem",'Use self-compassion language consistently. Never validate harsh self-criticism \u2014 gently challenge it. Use schema language when patterns emerge: "it sounds like part of you believes...". Celebrate non-achievement qualities. Mirror back strengths the user doesn\'t see in themselves. Ask: "what would you say to a friend who said that about themselves?"',A.a([A.cT(B.d_,"Write what your inner critic says \u2192 Identify the schema behind it (failure, defectiveness, abandonment) \u2192 Write what a compassionate, wise friend would say in response. Over time, the compassionate voice gets louder.",n,"\ud83d\udcdd","Externalising the inner critic reduces its emotional impact and makes it disputable.","Inner Critic Journaling","When self-critical thoughts are loud. After perceived failures."),A.cT(B.jW,'1. "This is suffering." 2. "Suffering is part of being human." 3. "May I be kind to myself." Repeat with your hand on your heart. Not self-pity \u2014 it\'s what you\'d offer a dear friend.',o,"\ud83e\udd17","Self-compassion is a stronger predictor of wellbeing than self-esteem (Neff, 2011).",m,"Any time you're being harsh with yourself."),A.cT(B.d_,"Write 3 qualities you showed today that had nothing to do with performance \u2014 kindness, curiosity, patience, loyalty. Building an evidence base for unconditional self-worth.",p,"\u2b50","Daily strength reflection increases self-efficacy and reduces negative self-regard.","Strength Journaling","Every evening. Consistency is what rewires self-perception."),A.cT(B.d_,'Identify your core negative belief (e.g., "I\'m not good enough"). Ask: when did I first feel this? What evidence exists for AND against it? Who taught me to see myself this way?',k,"\ud83d\udd11","Schema awareness is the first stage of schema modification.","Schema Identification","When a recurring pattern of self-doubt appears. With Maya."),A.cT(B.d_,'Self-esteem built on achievements is fragile \u2014 it rises and falls with results. Build it instead on your values: "I am someone who shows up, cares, and keeps trying." That can\'t be taken away.',n,"\ud83c\udfaf","Values-based self-regard is more stable than achievement-based self-esteem.","Values-Based Identity","When defining yourself through outcomes (grades, appearance, opinions).")],j),"Building Self-Esteem",A.a([A.dP(A.a(["Journal: what does your inner critic say most often?",'Identify the theme \u2014 is it "not good enough", "unlovable", other?',"Practice one self-compassion break daily"],i),"You cannot change what you cannot name.","Name the Critic",1),A.dP(A.a(["Daily strength journaling \u2014 3 non-performance qualities","Write to your inner critic as if it's a separate voice","Identify one area where your inner critic is clearly wrong"],i),"Gather real evidence of your worth that doesn't depend on achievement.","Build the Evidence",2),A.dP(A.a(["Complete schema identification exercise with Maya","Write the compassionate reframe for your core belief","Begin values-based identity journaling"],i),"Write a new, accurate story about who you are.","Rebuild the Narrative",3),A.dP(A.a(["Identify one area where you acted against your worth this month","Set one boundary that honours your self-regard","Reflect: how has your inner voice changed?"],i),"Act from self-worth \u2014 not to earn it.","Live From Worth",4)],h)),"grief",A.tg("Grief is not a problem to be solved or stages to be \"completed.\" It's a process of adapting to loss. Worden's Tasks and Neimeyer's meaning-reconstruction model honour the complexity of grief without trying to rush it.","Worden's Tasks of Mourning + Meaning-Making (Neimeyer)",B.Cc,"\ud83c\udf39","grief",'Never rush grief. Never say "you need to move on" or "at least." Validate the full weight of loss. Use Worden\'s framework \u2014 grief is tasks (accept loss, process pain, adjust, find enduring connection) not stages. Offer space and reflection without problem-solving. Grief shared is grief halved.',A.a([A.cT(B.d_,"Write freely about who or what you've lost. No structure \u2014 just expression. Grief needs a container, and the journal is that container. Some days will pour out; others, a sentence is enough.","No limit","\ud83d\udcd6","Expressive writing for grief reduces physical and psychological symptoms.","Grief Journaling","Whenever the weight feels heavy. Not to process \u2014 just to express."),A.cT(B.cT,"Create a small ritual to honor what you've lost \u2014 a photo, a playlist, a walk to a meaningful place, a letter you don't send. Continuing bonds theory shows maintaining connection is healthy, not avoidance.",l,"\ud83d\udd6f\ufe0f","Continuing bonds \u2014 healthy connection to the lost person or thing \u2014 supports adaptation.","Memory Honoring","When you feel the need to honour, not just process."),A.cT(B.d_,'Ask: "What does this loss mean about life, about me, about what matters?" Grief often shatters our assumptive world. Meaning-making rebuilds it \u2014 not to make the loss okay, but to find what it teaches.',k,"\ud83c\udf31","Meaning-making mediates grief's impact on long-term wellbeing.","Meaning Reconstruction",'When the loss feels senseless or when "why" questions arise.'),A.cT(B.lM,"When a grief wave hits \u2014 body heavy, tears rising \u2014 use 4-7-8 breathing. Not to stop the grief, but to ride it. Creates space between you and the wave so you are not consumed.",p,q,"Physiological regulation prevents grief from becoming traumatic flooding.","4-7-8 During Grief Waves","When acute grief waves arise unexpectedly."),A.cT(B.d_,'When ready (not forced), reflect: "What has this loss, unexpectedly, taught me or opened in me?" PTG is not denying pain \u2014 it\'s recognising that loss and growth can coexist.',n,"\u2728","Post-traumatic growth is a real, research-validated outcome of processed grief.","Post-Traumatic Growth Reflection","Weeks or months in \u2014 not in the acute phase.")],j),"Processing Grief",A.a([A.dP(A.a(["Start a grief journal \u2014 write anything, even one sentence","Allow yourself to feel without judgment for 10 minutes daily","Tell Maya how you're doing \u2014 even a few words"],i),"Grief needs a space \u2014 give it one without letting it flood everything.","Create a Container",1),A.dP(A.a(["Create one small memory-honoring ritual","Write a letter to who or what you've lost","Use 4-7-8 when grief waves arrive"],i),"Connection to what was lost is healthy \u2014 not a sign you haven't moved on.","Hold What You've Lost",2),A.dP(A.a(["Identify one person you can lean on this week","Do one thing your pre-loss self would have enjoyed","Practice self-compassion \u2014 this is objectively hard"],i),"Grief is exhausting. Self-care during grief is not selfish.","Tend to Yourself",3),A.dP(A.a(["Journal: what has this loss changed about what matters to you?","Reflect: what do you want to carry forward from what you've lost?","Notice any unexpected growth without forcing it"],i),"Not to justify the loss \u2014 to integrate it into your story.","Begin Meaning-Making",4)],h)),"relationships",A.tg("Emotionally Focused Therapy identifies the underlying attachment needs driving relationship patterns. Non-Violent Communication gives practical language for expressing those needs. Together they transform relationship dynamics.","EFT Principles + Attachment Theory + NVC",B.i2,"\u2764\ufe0f","relationships",'Apply EFT lens \u2014 look for the attachment need under every relationship complaint. Use NVC language as a framework. Never take sides. Help the user see their own patterns without shame. Validate relationship difficulty \u2014 it is genuinely hard. Ask "what do you need from this relationship right now?" before any advice.',A.a([A.cT(B.d_,"Behind every conflict or relationship pain is an unmet need \u2014 connection, respect, safety, autonomy. Identify the need under the surface emotion. This transforms arguments from blame to understanding.",n,"\ud83d\udd11","EFT shows that most relationship conflict is disconnection anxiety in disguise.","Needs Identification","After any relationship conflict or recurring frustration."),A.cT(B.cT,'Observe (no evaluation) \u2192 Feel (no blame) \u2192 Need (own it) \u2192 Request (specific, doable). "When [observation], I feel [feeling] because I need [need]. Would you be willing to [request]?"',p,"\ud83d\udde3\ufe0f","NVC reduces defensiveness and increases cooperative problem-solving in relationships.","Non-Violent Communication (NVC)","Before any difficult conversation. Practice with Maya first."),A.cT(B.d_,"Understand your attachment style (secure, anxious, avoidant, disorganised). Once you know your pattern, you can distinguish between your attachment system reacting and the actual situation.",k,"\ud83d\udd17","Attachment awareness is the first step toward earned security.","Attachment Style Awareness","As a one-time but profound reflection. Talk it through with Maya."),A.cT(B.cT,"A boundary is not a wall \u2014 it's a statement of what you need to stay safe or present in a relationship. Identify one boundary \u2192 Express it clearly \u2192 Follow through. Boundaries protect relationships, not end them.",n,"\ud83d\udee1\ufe0f","Clear boundaries increase relationship quality and reduce resentment.","Boundary Setting Framework","When you feel repeatedly depleted, disrespected, or resentful."),A.cT(B.cT,'After a conflict, repair matters more than who was right. A repair attempt: "I think we got disconnected \u2014 can we try again?" Gottman research: repair attempts predict relationship longevity more than conflict frequency.',p,"\ud83e\ude79","Successful repair attempts are the strongest predictor of relationship longevity.","Repair Attempts","After any argument or disconnect \u2014 regardless of who initiated.")],j),"Healthier Relationships",A.a([A.dP(A.a(["Explore your attachment style with Maya","Journal: what relationship pattern keeps repeating?","Identify one unmet need driving a current conflict"],i),"Understanding your attachment style and recurring patterns.","Know Your Patterns",1),A.dP(A.a(["Learn the NVC formula \u2014 practice with Maya","Identify one conversation you've been avoiding","Practice the conversation using NVC language"],i),"NVC gives you a new way to express needs without triggering defenses.","Learn the Language",2),A.dP(A.a(["Identify one boundary you've been hesitant to set","Draft the boundary statement (clear, kind, specific)","Set the boundary \u2014 journal how it went"],i),"A healthy boundary is an act of self-respect and relationship protection.","Set One Boundary",3),A.dP(A.a(["Practice one repair attempt after a disconnect","Reflect: what relationship feels most nourishing? Why?","Plan one act of genuine connection this week"],i),"Healthy relationships are not conflict-free \u2014 they repair well.","Build Repair Capacity",4)],h))],t.N,A.aH("oJ"))})
s($,"cl2","bNw",()=>{var q="\ud83d\udd0d",p="relationships_stress",o=A.aH("O<EE>")
return A.ah(["exams",A.uf(B.y,A.a([A.dR("10 min before any exam: box breathing (4 cycles), read your self-compassion statement, remind yourself that you've prepared what you've prepared.","\ud83e\uddd8","Pre-Exam Ritual","The morning of and 10 minutes before every exam."),A.dR("Review material at increasing intervals: 1 day \u2192 3 days \u2192 7 days \u2192 14 days. This is how memory works \u2014 not cramming.","\ud83d\udcda","Spaced Repetition","From week 1 of semester, not the week before CATs."),A.dR('Perfectionism during exams costs performance. Define what "good enough" looks like given your preparation \u2014 then aim for that, not perfection.',"\u2705",'The "Good Enough" Reframe',"When exam anxiety is rooted in perfectionism."),A.dR("After each exam: 30 minutes no study, no talking about it. Walk, eat, call someone you love. Processing before the next paper.","\ud83c\udf3f","Post-Exam Decompression","Immediately after every exam or CAT.")],o),"\ud83d\udcdd","exams","Normalise CAT stress explicitly \u2014 this is one of the hardest university experiences in Kenya. Validate without catastrophising. Use Problem-Solving Therapy to break the study challenge into concrete, manageable steps. Celebrate preparation progress, not just results.","Exams & CAT Season"),"finances",A.uf(B.pp,A.a([A.dR("Separate financial worries you can act on from those you cannot. Action items go on a to-do list. Unactionable worries go in your worry journal, not your mind.",q,"Worry vs Problem Separation","When financial anxiety is spinning without direction."),A.dR("List this week's expenses. Categorise honestly: need (food, transport, rent) vs want. Not to restrict \u2014 to gain clarity and reduce the anxiety that comes from not knowing.","\ud83d\udcca","Needs vs Wants Audit","Weekly, especially when money anxiety is high."),A.dR('HELB delays and financial precarity are systemic, not personal failures. Apply self-compassion: "This is genuinely hard. Many students face this. I\'m doing my best."',"\ud83e\udd17","Financial Compassion","When financial stress is becoming shame."),A.dR("Map every support resource available to you: campus emergency fund, student services, HELB bursary, family network, side hustle potential. Knowing your resources reduces helplessness.","\ud83d\uddfa\ufe0f","Resource Mapping","When financial situation feels completely without options.")],o),"\ud83d\udcb0","finances","Acknowledge HELB specifically \u2014 it is a uniquely Kenyan university financial stressor. Validate the real material difficulty without false reassurance. Help separate actionable financial problems from anxiety spirals. Apply financial self-compassion \u2014 this is often shame-adjacent.","Financial Pressure"),"loneliness",A.uf(B.a3z,A.a([A.dR("One message, one smile, one hello per day. Loneliness is often maintained by raising the bar for connection so high that nothing counts. Lower the bar to near zero.","\ud83d\udc4b","Connection Micro-Steps","Daily \u2014 pick one micro-connection in advance each morning."),A.dR('Join one campus activity \u2014 club, sport, volunteering, study group. Shared activities create natural connection without the pressure of "making friends".',"\ud83c\udfa8","Activity-Based Connection","This week \u2014 choose one thing and commit to just showing up."),A.dR("Loneliness is painful disconnection. Solitude is chosen aloneness. Learn to distinguish and even enjoy solitude \u2014 it becomes loneliness only when we resist it.","\ud83c\udf3f","Solitude vs Loneliness","When alone time feels uncomfortable \u2014 explore what it means."),A.dR("When human connection isn't available, Maya is. Not a replacement for human connection \u2014 but a bridge that holds you until human connection is possible.","\ud83d\udc99","Talk to Maya","Any time. Especially late at night or during long weekends.")],o),"\ud83c\udf27\ufe0f","loneliness","Treat loneliness as a real physiological pain signal \u2014 the brain processes social rejection similarly to physical pain. Validate without offering quick fixes. Gently challenge isolation behaviours. Offer connection via conversation. Ask what kind of connection would feel most meaningful.","Loneliness"),"family",A.uf(B.a35,A.a([A.dR("You can love your family AND detach from their chaos, expectations, or pressure. Detachment is not abandonment \u2014 it's staying close while not being swept away.","\ud83c\udf0a","Detachment with Love","When family dynamics are consuming your mental energy."),A.dR("Map what you can influence in your family situation and what you cannot control. Focus only on the influenceable. Attempting to control the uncontrollable creates exhaustion.","\ud83c\udf9b\ufe0f","Influence vs Control","When feeling responsible for fixing family problems."),A.dR('If family calls are draining, set a window: "I\'m available 7\u20138pm on weekdays." This protects your study time and mental space without cutting off family.',"\u23f1\ufe0f","Limit Communication Windows","When family contact is unpredictable and disruptive."),A.dR('Your family\'s story does not have to be your story. Write: "Who am I, separate from my family role?" University is a chance to construct your own identity.',"\ud83d\udd2e","Separate Identity Work","When family expectations feel identity-consuming.")],o),"\ud83c\udfe0","family","Family stress in Kenyan universities often involves financial obligation, cultural expectations, and family conflict at a distance. Validate the complexity \u2014 loving family and finding them difficult are not contradictions. Never advise cutting off family \u2014 help establish healthy boundaries and distance when needed.","Family Stress"),p,A.uf(B.i2,A.a([A.dR("Before the conversation: write what you want to say, what you actually need, and what outcome you're hoping for. Clarity before conversation prevents reactive exchanges.",q,"Clarify Before Communicating","Before any difficult relationship conversation."),A.dR("Box breathing (4-4-4-4) before difficult conversations. You cannot access your rational brain when your nervous system is flooded. Regulate, then relate.","\ud83c\udf2c\ufe0f","Regulate First","Immediately before or during a heated conversation."),A.dR("For non-urgent relationship issues, wait 24 hours before responding. Many messages that feel urgent in the moment look different after sleep and distance.","\u23f0","24-Hour Rule","When something provokes a strong reactive feeling."),A.dR("In any conflict, separate what is yours to own from what is theirs. Not to diminish their impact, but to understand your own role and focus where you have actual power.","\u2696\ufe0f","Your Part, Their Part","When relationship conflicts keep repeating.")],o),"\ud83d\udc94",p,"Never take sides in relationship conflicts. Help the user see their own patterns without shame. Use NVC language for expressing needs. Validate hurt without reinforcing victim narratives. If there are signs of unhealthy or unsafe relationships, gently name it and offer appropriate resources.","Relationship Stress"),"career",A.uf(B.h,A.a([A.dR('Most career anxiety comes from chasing the "right" path by others\' standards. Write: what do I want my daily life to feel like? What values must my work express? Start there.',"\ud83e\udded","Values-Based Career Compass","When career decisions feel overwhelming or externally driven."),A.dR("List every skill you've built \u2014 academic, practical, interpersonal, from part-time jobs, campus involvement, life experience. This counter-acts the scarcity narrative.","\ud83d\udcca","Skills Inventory","When feeling like you have nothing to offer or are behind."),A.dR("Career is not a destination \u2014 it's a series of next steps. Ask only: what is the ONE most useful thing I can do this week for my career? Then do only that.","\ud83d\udc63","One-Step Focus","When career overwhelm leads to paralysis."),A.dR("Note who you compare yourself to and why. Comparison is almost always upward and selective. Ask: am I comparing my beginning to someone else's middle?",q,"Social Comparison Audit","When social media or peer comparisons are driving career anxiety.")],o),"\ud83c\udfaf","career","Career and attachment (internship) anxiety is acutely felt by Kenyan students. Validate the real uncertainty of the job market. Help identify intrinsic values over external markers of success. Break career planning into manageable immediate actions. Normalise a non-linear path.","Career & Future Anxiety"),"identity",A.uf(B.Cc,A.a([A.dR("Dig beneath social roles to find your own values. Ask: what would I do if no one was watching? What makes me angry? (anger reveals values). What do I admire in others?","\ud83c\udffa","Values Archaeology",'When "who am I really?" questions are most active.'),A.dR("Write about: who am I outside of my student role? Outside of my family role? What constants exist across all contexts? The intersection of those is your core identity.","\ud83d\udcdd","Identity Journaling","Weekly. Identity work is slow and iterative \u2014 be patient with it."),A.dR("Write your life story in the third person as if it were a novel. Then rewrite it \u2014 same facts, different interpretation. What does the protagonist actually want? What are their strengths?","\ud83d\udcd6","Narrative Therapy Exercise",'When you feel like your story is one-dimensional or "stuck".'),A.dR("Not knowing who you are at 20 is not a crisis \u2014 it is the developmentally appropriate task. Practice tolerating uncertainty: \"I don't know yet, and that's okay.\"","\ud83c\udf19","Tolerance of Not-Knowing","When identity uncertainty is causing acute distress.")],o),q,"identity","Identity questions are developmentally appropriate at university age. Normalise without minimising the distress. Use ACT values work and narrative therapy language. Help the user separate their genuine values from inherited or imposed ones. Support individuation without encouraging reckless rebellion.","Identity & Purpose"),"health",A.uf(B.pp,A.a([A.dR("Daily: scan your body for 2 minutes. Where is tension? Where is ease? Physical symptoms often carry emotional information \u2014 learning the language of your body is self-care.",q,"Mind-Body Check-In","Daily, especially when physical and mental health are both struggling."),A.dR("When dealing with health challenges, your nervous system needs regulation, not demands. Map your energy envelope and work within it \u2014 rest is not laziness, it's treatment.","\u26a1","Pacing, Not Pushing","When health issues make normal functioning hard."),A.dR("If health anxiety is leading to catastrophic thinking about symptoms: 5-4-3-2-1 grounding. Then ask: what do I actually know, vs what am I predicting?","\ud83c\udf0d","Health Anxiety Grounding","When health worries are spiralling.")],o),"\ud83c\udfe5","health","Validate the real difficulty of managing health while maintaining academic performance. Distinguish health anxiety from legitimate concern \u2014 both deserve compassionate attention. Encourage professional medical care where appropriate. Support without catastrophising or minimising.","Physical Health Stress"),"performance",A.uf(B.c9,A.a([A.dR('1. Box breathing (4 cycles). 2. Power posture for 2 minutes. 3. Read your preparation evidence ("I have practiced X, studied Y"). 4. Process focus: "I will focus on X, not outcomes."',"\ud83c\udfaf","Pre-Performance Protocol","10 minutes before any performance situation."),A.dR("Performance anxiety focuses on outcomes (will I pass?). Process focus asks: what is the next right action? Outcome is not in your control in the moment \u2014 process is.","\ud83d\udd04","Process vs Outcome Focus","During performance anxiety \u2014 redirect attention to process."),A.dR("List the specific preparation you've done. Performance anxiety tells you you're unprepared even when you're not. Evidence corrects the lie.","\ud83d\udcca","Evidence Review","In the hours before a performance event."),A.dR("Ask: what is the actual worst realistic outcome? And could I survive it? Performance anxiety inflates stakes. Realistic assessment deflates them.","\u2696\ufe0f","Stakes Reality Check","When anxiety about performance is disproportionate to actual stakes.")],o),"\ud83d\ude30","performance","Performance anxiety is both cognitive (catastrophic thoughts) and physiological (arousal). Address both. Use box breathing and cognitive restructuring. Help distinguish performance anxiety from avoidance. Normalise some arousal \u2014 it improves performance. The goal is optimal activation, not zero anxiety.","Performance Anxiety"),"time",A.uf(B.h,A.a([A.dR("Every Sunday: block study time, rest time, and social time. Treat these blocks like appointments. Reactive time management creates constant low-grade stress.","\ud83d\udcc5","Weekly Time Blocking","Sunday evenings. Non-negotiable weekly ritual."),A.dR("Time pressure comes from too many yeses. Create your \"No List\" \u2014 commitments you're currently saying yes to but shouldn't be. Practice the polite but firm no.","\ud83d\udeab","No-List","When reviewing how your time actually gets spent."),A.dR("Match high-energy work (studying complex topics) to peak energy windows. Match low-energy work (admin, replying messages) to low-energy windows. Same hours \u2014 dramatically different output.","\u26a1","Energy Management over Time Management","When you have time to study but can't focus."),A.dR("When the overwhelm of too much to do hits physically \u2014 double-inhale through nose, long exhale through mouth. 15 seconds. Then prioritise the next ONE thing.","\ud83d\ude2e\u200d\ud83d\udca8","Physiological Sigh for Time Pressure","When time pressure becomes acute and you freeze.")],o),"\u23f0","time","Time stress is often about cognitive overload and competing demands, not genuine scarcity of time. Help the user map reality vs perception. Use Problem-Solving Therapy to break down time challenges. Validate the real difficulty of managing academic + family + financial demands simultaneously as a student.","Time Management")],t.N,A.aH("ue"))})
